@vsm 1.0
// thermal_resource_governance_amendment_2026-08-02.vsm
// Author: Claude [O] + rmw3 (S5), 2026-08-02.
// Grew from a single question ("can you check n02's temperature?") into a real
// architecture study spanning the Ω/covenant boundary, S3's resource bargain, the
// A5 autonomy ceiling, and a live cgroup enforcement gap. rmw3: "creo que este arco
// abre mas preguntas, pero por ahora esta bien... documentar y atacar esto cuando
// tengamos la ventana abierta." Status: DOCUMENTED, NOT DISPATCHED — no window yet.

spec(THERMAL-RESOURCE-GOVERNANCE-AMENDMENT) {

  status: "Study only. Nothing here is built beyond the thermal watchdog v1
      (already shipped same session — see task(THERMAL-WATCHDOG-MISSING) in
      TASKS.vsm, status=partial). Everything below waits for an open work
      window — explicitly not urgent-dispatch, per rmw3.",

  // ── THREAD 1: is temperature an Ω/ℝ¹²⁸ dimension? Answer: no, and shouldn't be ──
  thread_1_omega_mapping: {
    question_rmw3: "la temperatura no es una categoria dentro de R^128 que de
        cuenta de la viabilidad del sistema? no esta mapeado?",
    confirmed: "NOT mapped, deliberately. covenant_ops.vsm:40 Ax_HardwareCeiling
        excludes thermal_sensor (with firmware, MMU) from Ω_VSF governance.
        hardware_ingest.rs samples real CPU temp into φ↑ but a dedicated test
        (cpu_temp_is_a_blind_spot_no_dim) proves by design it never writes a
        state dimension or feeds Ω/S3/S4/S5 decisions.",
    debate: "[O]'s first framing (temperature as a binary 'categorical gate' —
        the DangerFace::Skip pattern already used elsewhere in
        omega_validator.rs, invalid/valid but not weighted) was too blunt.
        rmw3 pushed back correctly: what actually helped today was a GRADUATED
        reading (85C, elevated but not extreme) prompting investigation, not a
        binary trip. A hard gate would have given either nothing (85C isn't
        'invalid') or an all-or-nothing veto — neither matches what worked.",
    resolution_direction: "Temperature likely belongs as an INPUT TO S3's
        RESOURCE BARGAIN (vsf-s3-alloc), not as an Ω/ℝ¹²⁸ dimension at all —
        a physical resource constraint alongside CPU/RAM/disk/network (see
        thread 2), informing graduated grant decisions, not a coherence/
        viability signal. This may mean Ax_HardwareCeiling's LETTER doesn't
        even need to change (it excludes thermal_sensor from Ω_VSF
        specifically) — what needs deciding is whether S3's resource-bargain
        function counts as 'Ω_VSF governance' in the axiom's scope, or is a
        genuinely separate control loop. THIS BOUNDARY IS THE OPEN QUESTION,
        not resolved here.",
  },

  // ── THREAD 2: graduated resource response — TIER 3 extended with thermal ──
  thread_2_s3_bargain_integration: {
    proposal: "Extends the TIER 3 resource-pool study already registered
        today (docs/spec/relay/s1_isolation_relay_arch_2026-08-02.vsm
        decision_4 — disk + network beyond the existing CPU/RAM TIER1/2
        model). Thermal headroom becomes a 4th dimension S3 can react to,
        same actuator-adapter-behind-shared-policy pattern already proposed
        there. NOT folded into Ω — a resource-bargain input, structurally
        identical to how S3 already reasons about the CPU/RAM pool.",
    escalation_ladder: [
      "WARN (shipped) -- log + state file, no action. Matches today's actual
       85C spike: informative, not actuating.",
      "ELEVATED, sustained + rising -- reduce grants to non-critical S1s.
       Does NOT cross A5 -- this is resize, the exact authority
       vsf-s3-alloc already has.",
      "CRITICAL, sustained + rising, approaching hardware throttle -- see
       thread 3, this is where it gets contested.",
    ],
  },

  // ── THREAD 3: the A5 question — emergency kill of hungry S1s ──────────────
  thread_3_a5_emergency_exception: {
    proposal_rmw3: "si el humano no está disponible y el consumo de recursos
        se sigue disparando, el sistema podría tomar medidas de emergencia
        para proteger el núcleo -- matar S1s hambrientos antes de que el CPU
        entre a un estado forzado de throttle/idle.",
    the_real_tension: "vsf-s3-alloc's coupling_registry entry already states
        explicitly: 'single-loop (A5: no LXC start/stop/create/destroy)'.
        Killing an S1 is a stop action -- this is not a new decision in a
        vacuum, it is reopening a boundary that was already deliberately
        drawn. A5 (CLAUDE.md): 'double-loop corrections ALWAYS require human
        approval.'",
    the_case_for_an_exception: "Physical safety + time-criticality is
        exactly what Beer's algedonic channel exists to bypass normal
        hierarchy for. If rmw3 is unavailable and thermal trend is
        genuinely worsening toward hardware damage, waiting for human
        approval may not be survivable in the available time window --
        analogous to a rev limiter, not a policy decision.",
    the_case_against_full_automation: "Killing a specific S1 has
        consequences beyond the immediate physical-safety question
        (interrupted work, unclear recovery, which S1 counts as
        'expendable' and by what criteria) -- exactly the kind of judgment
        A5 was written to keep on the human side.",
    live_case_study_2026_08_02: "The exact scenario played out for real,
        manually, same session: n02 load stuck at 30+ for hours, DS's
        SOC-STAGE-A-PHASE1-WAZUH-MINIMAL-INSTALL badly I/O-starved. rmw3
        stopped 111 (s1-bbh-lab), 113 (s1-econ), 114 (jupyter, outside the
        VSM), 108 (dev-sandbox), 109 (s5-opsroom) -- and explicitly KEPT
        112 (s1-clima) running despite it being, at one point, the single
        largest CPU consumer on the host. This is the concrete proof the
        open questions below matter, not a hypothetical: a naive
        autonomous rule like 'stop whatever is consuming the most' would
        have picked clima FIRST and gotten it exactly backwards -- resource
        consumption and expendability are NOT the same axis. What actually
        happened was a live, context-full human judgment call ('clima vale
        la pena mantenerlo'), not execution of a pre-declared rule. Also
        notable: the stops crossed scope boundaries the S3-S1 bargain
        framing doesn't cover -- 108/109 aren't S1 at all (tooling / S5
        presentation layer), 114 isn't part of the VSM. And the response
        skipped straight to full stop, never tried the ELEVATED
        (reduce-grants) tier first -- given how long the contention had
        already been sustained, jumping tiers may itself be the correct
        move under real urgency, not a deviation from the design.",
    open_questions_not_resolved: [
      "Is CRITICAL-tier kill single-loop (automatable, bounded, physically
       necessitated) or double-loop (requires human, no exception)?",
      "If an exception is ratified: does kill fire immediately at CRITICAL,
       or does it page a human first (algedonic) with kill as fallback only
       after a grace window with no response?",
      "Which S1s/units are 'expendable' in this scenario, decided how --
       PROVEN 2026-08-02 this cannot be resource-consumption-based (clima
       counterexample above). Needs an explicit, RATIFIED priority policy
       declared in advance (e.g. nucleus > broker > audit > S4 > clima >
       econ > bbh-lab > tooling/jupyter), not a judgment made live --
       the whole point of automating this is for when no one is available
       to make that live call.",
      "Does the same priority policy also need to authorize scope beyond
       S1 (108/109-style tooling, non-VSM boxes like jupyter) under a
       broader 'host emergency protection' banner, since the live case
       needed exactly that and the S3-S1 bargain framing alone doesn't
       cover it?",
      "Should the escalation ladder allow skipping straight to a stronger
       tier when contention has already been severe and sustained, rather
       than always stepping through ELEVATED first? The live case suggests
       yes but this isn't decided.",
      "Exact CRITICAL threshold (some margin below the ~95-100C silicon
       throttle -- not fixed here).",
    ],
  },

  // ── THREAD 4: cgroup enforcement gap — found investigating thread 3 ────────
  thread_4_cgroup_gap: {
    question_rmw3: "si un proceso o S1 está hambriento de recursos,
        consumiendo 99%, el cgroup también debería haber operado, limitando
        el consumo y avisando con una señal que tampoco recibí.",
    confirmed_live_2026_08_02: [
      "LXC 101 (cores:4) and 111 (cores:2, no cpulimit): live cgroup
       cpu.max reads literally 'max' (unlimited) despite pct config
       implying a core allocation -- 'cores:' sets affinity/visible count,
       NOT a hard cgroup quota; that needs 'cpulimit', which neither has.",
      "Both: live memory.max also reads 'max' -- no hard cap, despite
       pct config showing memory:3072/4096.",
      "Both: io.max is EMPTY -- zero I/O throttling configured anywhere,
       and I/O was the actual bottleneck in today's whole incident.",
      "LXC 107 DOES have cpulimit:1 configured in pct config -- but its
       live cgroup cpu.max ALSO reads 'max'. Unexplained -- either the
       wrong cgroup path was checked, Proxmox applies the limit via a
       different mechanism (e.g. cpu.weight, not cpu.max), or the
       container needs a restart to pick up a config change made after
       boot. NOT resolved, flagged honestly as needing real investigation
       rather than a guessed explanation.",
      "PSI (pressure stall information) IS available and accumulating real
       data -- cpu.pressure and io.pressure both show nonzero historical
       'total' stall time -- but NOTHING reads or alerts on it. The
       exact signal rmw3 expected exists at the kernel level with zero
       consumers wired to it.",
    ],
    why_this_matters_more_than_thermal: "This is arguably the more
        foundational gap of the two -- proper cgroup enforcement + PSI-based
        alerting would have caught today's actual root cause (the
        aggregator.py cron cascade eating CPU/IO) directly, not just a
        downstream symptom like elevated temperature. Explicitly kept
        UNIFIED with threads 1-3 per rmw3 ('mantengamoslo unido, es parte de
        lo mismo') rather than split into a separate arc.",
    second_live_case_study_2026_08_02_morning: "The same night's SOC-dashboard
        saga produced a second, sharper proof of this gap, hours after the
        first. Measured via cgroup io.stat before stopping clima: s1-clima
        (112) had written ~1.06GB across 272,684 individual write ops
        (cumulative) -- ~38x the write volume AND ~38x the write-op count of
        the LXC (117) actually being starved. Root cause: 112 runs 83 of the
        host's 113 total Python processes (73x l3_node.py + 8x l2_zone.py +
        2x l1_system.py -- see task(CLIMA-PROCESS-FOOTPRINT-STUDY)), each
        appending its own .jsonl telemetry log continuously -- 87 separate
        write streams, brutal for a spinning disk's IOPS ceiling regardless
        of raw bytes. rmw3's framing, verbatim and worth keeping exactly:
        'no puede ser que un solo S1 se consuma toda la capacidad de
        escritura de un sistema, esto rompe la viabilidad del sistema y
        debería haber sido un cruce de frontera.' This reframes io.max from
        'a nice-to-have PSI-adjacent gap' to a genuine VIABILITY defect: one
        unit was able to unboundedly consume a shared finite resource with
        zero counter-mechanism, degrading system-wide capability (blocked
        DS's SOC work 12+ hours) with nothing detecting or containing it --
        precisely the S2 (units-stepping-on-each-other) / S3
        (resource-allocation-with-synergy) failure mode Beer's model exists
        to prevent, not a peripheral efficiency question. Concrete
        implication for the eventual fix: io.max per-unit quotas are not
        optional polish, they are the actual boundary that was missing.
        Clima was manually stopped (rmw3 confirmed, reversible) as the
        immediate mitigation -- this finding is about the missing structural
        mechanism, not clima's guilt specifically; any S1 could have done
        this to any other, with nothing to stop it.",
    technical_footgun_io_weight_vs_bfq: "Found live the same night, trying
        to prioritize the LXC (117) actually being starved: cgroup v2's
        `io.weight` is a COMPLETE NO-OP unless the block device's active
        I/O scheduler is BFQ -- n02's `sda` was on `mq-deadline` (the
        default), so hours of trying to prioritize 117's stuck dpkg unpack
        via `io.weight` did nothing at all, silently -- no error, no
        warning, the setting simply has zero effect under any other
        scheduler. `cpu.weight` has no such dependency (works regardless of
        I/O scheduler) -- this is specific to I/O. The scheduler is
        hot-swappable live (`echo bfq > /sys/block/sda/queue/scheduler`, no
        reboot, instant, reversible) -- switched it live as a test, but
        even with BFQ active the specific stuck process (PID 5787, wchan
        `submit_bio_wait`) never moved, suggesting the real bottleneck was
        raw disk contention / a pending fsync, not schedulable priority at
        all -- no kernel hung-task warning fired either. rmw3's call: stop
        intervening, let it run overnight -- this became the loudest
        practical argument for the SSD migration
        (project_proxmox_vm_architecture memory) being a real fix rather
        than a nice-to-have. IMPLICATION for any future io.max/io.weight
        enforcement work (thread 4's own proposed fix direction): verify
        which scheduler is active FIRST -- a correctly-configured
        io.weight on mq-deadline is silently inert, not a smaller effect,
        zero effect.",
    third_symptom_wazuh_db_d_state_2026_08_02: "A third live instance of
        this same root cause, found later the same session while enrolling
        Wazuh agents (see project_soc_perimeter_extension memory /
        TASKS.vsm SOC-PERIMETER-EXTENSION-N01-WSL-AGENTS): `wazuh-db` on
        LXC 116 (SOC manager) was observed in kernel state `D`
        (uninterruptible sleep, blocked on disk I/O) -- its own unix
        socket (`queue/db/wdb`) intermittently vanished and reappeared
        under load, causing agent group-assignment to fail in a tight
        retry loop and new Wazuh agents to stay stuck at 'Unknown' status
        even with a fully healthy TCP connection. Not a Wazuh bug --
        confirmed the same single-spinning-disk contention as every other
        symptom in this file, just surfacing in a new service that had
        never been stressed this way before (n01's agent was literally the
        first external enrollment since SOC Phase 1 install). Same
        prescription as everywhere else here: don't chase it as a
        standalone SOC defect, recheck once the SSD migration lands.",
  },

  // ── THREAD 5: hardware telemetry inventory — captured vs. acted on ────────
  thread_5_hardware_telemetry_inventory: {
    question_rmw3: "seria bueno hacer un inventario de la telemetria critica
        del hardware y ver que esta siendo considerada y que no, e
        integrarlo a la matriz.",
    method: "Live-checked every signal below on n02 directly (sysfs, dmesg,
        journalctl, smartctl, netdata's own API) rather than assuming from
        docs. CPU temp and the cgroup cpu.max/memory.max/io.max gap are NOT
        re-derived here -- already thoroughly covered in threads 1-4, just
        cited.",
    the_headline_finding: "netdata (already running on n02, confirmed via
        its own API: 5778 charts total) is SILENTLY ALREADY CAPTURING most
        of what looked like total gaps -- PSI (system.cpu_some_pressure,
        system.io_some_pressure, system.memory_some_pressure and their
        _stall_time variants), disk I/O (disk_busy.sda, disk_util.sda,
        disk_iotime.sda, disk_ops.sda, disk_qops.sda), memory/OOM
        (mem.oom_kill, mem.available, mem.swap). This changes the shape of
        the fix for most of these: the gap is 'nothing reads netdata's API
        and acts on it', not 'nothing captures the data' -- a much cheaper
        problem than building new capture mechanisms from scratch.",
    matrix: [
      "CPU temp -- CAPTURED (hwmon, coretemp) / LOGGED (state file) / ALERTED (vsf-thermal-watchdog, shipped this session) / GOVERNS: nothing (by design, Ax_HardwareCeiling -- see thread 1).",
      "CPU cgroup limits (cpu.max) -- NOT CAPTURED as a limit (reads 'max' everywhere) / N/A logged / N/A alerted / GOVERNS: nothing -- see thread 4, not re-derived here.",
      "Memory cgroup limits (memory.max) -- same as CPU: NOT CAPTURED as a real limit / GOVERNS: nothing -- see thread 4.",
      "io.max per-unit quota -- NOT CAPTURED, confirmed empty everywhere -- see thread 4's second live case study (clima). The single highest-priority gap of everything in this inventory, already P1.",
      "CPU throttle events -- CAPTURED (sysfs thermal_throttle counters, live-checked) -- core_throttle_count=5571, package_throttle_count=5571, core_throttle_total_time_ms=62863 (~63s cumulative throttled time over 100+ days uptime, ~55 events/day average) -- NEW FINDING, never surfaced before this inventory. NOT logged with history, NOT alerted, GOVERNS nothing. Worth a follow-up: is 5571 events normal background noise for this hardware's whole lifetime, or does it correlate with today's load spikes? Not established either way.",
      "Disk SMART health (/dev/sda) -- CAPTURED (smartctl works) -- overall PASSED, but Reallocated_Sector_Ct=12 (NOT zero -- a real, if modest, wear indicator on a disk that has been under sustained heavy load this whole session), Seek_Error_Rate raw=4592, Power_On_Hours=19110 (~2.2 years), Current_Pending_Sector=0, Offline_Uncorrectable=0. NOT logged with trend history anywhere found, NOT alerted, GOVERNS nothing. Given this exact disk is the bottleneck the whole session revolved around and is getting replaced by the planned SSD migration, this is lower urgency than it would otherwise be -- but the 12 reallocated sectors are non-zero and worth a mental note, not necessarily a new task.",
      "Disk I/O stats (throughput, busy%, queue depth) -- CAPTURED continuously by netdata (disk_busy.sda/disk_util.sda/disk_iotime.sda/disk_ops.sda/disk_qops.sda) -- this whole session's ad hoc /proc/diskstats and cgroup io.stat checks were manually reinventing what netdata already tracks with history. NOT alerted, GOVERNS nothing. Cheapest fix in this whole inventory: wire an alert to netdata's existing data, don't build new capture.",
      "Disk space -- CAPTURED (df point-in-time, and almost certainly a netdata chart too though not explicitly enumerated) -- currently 61G/94G free (33% used), healthy. NO cron/timer/persistent trend-watch found -- someone has to remember to check. Not urgent at 33% used, but this exact class of problem (LVM-thin near-full) already bit the project once before per memory project_algedonic_storage_blindspot -- a cheap watchdog here would be proportionate, not paranoid.",
      "Memory / swap / OOM -- CAPTURED (free, netdata mem.* charts including mem.oom_kill) -- 23G total/18G available, swap 11G total/1.1G used, healthy. Checked dmesg + journalctl -k directly for OOM-kill history: NONE found. NOT alerted proactively (no threshold watcher), GOVERNS nothing, but the underlying data already exists in netdata -- same cheap-fix shape as disk I/O.",
      "Network interface (vmbr0) -- CAPTURED (ip -s link, netdata ipv4/ipv6/net charts) -- RX 4 dropped / TX 12 dropped / 0 errors, cumulative over billions of packets -- negligible, healthy. NOT alerted, GOVERNS nothing. Lowest-priority item in this inventory -- no evidence of a real problem here.",
      "Battery / AC power -- CAPTURED only via raw sysfs (/sys/class/power_supply/, BAT0 present, capacity=96%, status=Not charging at check time; AC present) -- confirms this IS real laptop hardware running 24/7 infra on a battery that can drain. `upower` isn't even installed, NOTHING reads this programmatically, NOT logged, NOT alerted, GOVERNS nothing. This is a genuine, literal single-point-of-failure risk (AC disconnect -> battery drain -> host dies, taking every LXC with it) that nothing in the stack currently watches for at all -- arguably the most concretely dangerous blind spot in this whole inventory precisely because it's a hard binary failure (host gone), not a graceful degradation like the others.",
      "Fan / cooling telemetry -- NOT AVAILABLE, not just uncaptured -- checked every hwmon device on this host (coretemp, acpitz, pch_skylake, hp, AC, BAT0), none expose a fan1_input. This laptop's firmware/hardware doesn't surface fan RPM via the standard kernel interface. Not a gap to fix -- there's nothing to read.",
    ],
    confirmed_governs_column: "Re-confirms thread 1/4's own claims rather than
        assuming them: across every signal checked in this inventory, the
        ONLY one with any downstream reaction at all is CPU temp (the new
        watchdog, and that's ALERT not GOVERN -- it still doesn't feed
        Ω/S3/S4/S5 per Ax_HardwareCeiling). Nothing else in this matrix is
        captured-and-alerted, let alone governs an actual decision. Confirms
        thread 4's framing was not overstated for CPU/mem/io.max, and
        extends the same finding to CPU-throttle, disk-SMART, disk-space,
        network, and (most concretely) battery/AC.",
  },

  fourth_symptom_became_its_own_incident_2026_08_02_night: "A FOURTH
      instance of this same host-I/O-contention root cause (following
      thread_4's clima case study, the io.weight/BFQ footgun, and the
      wazuh-db D-state symptom above) was found the same night: NATS on
      LXC 106 degraded (systemd D-Bus 'Transport endpoint is not
      connected', accept queue saturated) during a window netdata confirms
      had disk_util.sda averaging 83.6% + io_some_pressure ~65-75%, vs a
      ~46% baseline a few hours earlier. This one grew into its own
      full incident response (crash-looped vsf-s4-clima-bridge for ~22h,
      a 7-week-broken watchdog, a leaked+rotated NATS token, BBS
      decommission, the legacy firewall=1 egress migration) -- NOT
      re-narrated here, full record:
      docs/incidents/2026-08-02_clima_nats_observability_incident.vsm +
      docs/incidents/2026-08-02_recovery_plan.vsm. Cross-linking so this
      thread's own reader sees the throughline: FOUR separate symptoms of
      the identical single-spinning-disk bottleneck, surfacing in FOUR
      unrelated-looking subsystems (a stuck dpkg unpack, SOC agent
      enrollment, NATS itself), across one continuous session. The
      recovery plan's own phase B/C (docs/incidents/2026-08-02_
      recovery_plan.vsm) and this file's thread 4 are the same underlying
      gap approached from two entry points -- coordinate scope before
      dispatching either, don't build the cgroup/io.max fix twice.",

  next: "No dispatch yet. When a work window opens: (1) resolve the
      Ax_HardwareCeiling-vs-S3-bargain scope question (thread 1) --
      probably the right FIRST step since it determines whether anything
      else here needs a covenant amendment or not. (2) design the graduated
      escalation ladder concretely (thread 2), including the actual
      threshold values. (3) get an explicit A5 ruling on emergency kill
      (thread 3) BEFORE building any kill-capable code, not after. (4)
      investigate + fix the cgroup limit gap (thread 4) -- likely the
      most mechanically straightforward of the four, and probably worth
      doing somewhat independently of the others given how directly it
      would have prevented today's actual incident.",
}
